course: dictionary
lesson: 5738
title: Lotto: a game of chance
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which meaning the writer means. This is the context. «Lotto» is one of these words.
explain: Read «In the UK's main Lotto the chance of the jackpot is about 1 in 45 million for each ticket.» The clues are chance, ticket and jackpot. A jackpot is the top prize, and you buy a ticket to try for it, so here lotto means a lottery game.
explain: The same word can also name a children's game. Like bingo, you cover pictures or numbers on your card when they are called out. To check your meaning, put it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: «Lotto» means a lottery game: people buy tickets with numbers, and if their numbers are drawn they win money.
fact: «Lotto» can also mean a children's game, like bingo, where you cover pictures or numbers on a card when they are called out.
fact: The top prize in a lottery is often called the jackpot.
fact: The chance of winning the top prize can be tiny: in the UK's main Lotto it is about 1 in 45 million for each ticket.
fact: Chance is studied in maths as probability, which measures how likely something is to happen.
exercise: «In the UK's main Lotto the chance of the jackpot is about 1 in 45 million.» What does lotto mean here? | A race where you run around a track | *A lottery game where people buy tickets with numbers | A card game played with a full deck of cards | A kind of pudding served at school
exercise: In a children's game, you cover numbers on your card when they are called out. Which game is this? | Hopscotch | Hide and seek | Football | *Lotto
exercise: «Ravi says the chance of winning the lotto is tiny, about one in many millions.» What does this tell you about winning? | Winning happens every single day | Winning is very likely | Winning is guaranteed for everyone who plays | *Winning is very unlikely
test: «Adults often talk about winning the lotto.» What does this most likely mean? | Reading a map of the town | Losing a game of hopscotch | *Winning money in a lottery game | Finishing first in a long race
test: Which is true about the children's game lotto? | You must run to the finish line before anyone else | You win by reading the most books | *You cover pictures or numbers on a card when they are called out | You throw dice and move a counter around a board
exam: «Mum checked her lotto ticket on Saturday night, hoping her numbers had been drawn.» What does lotto mean in this sentence? | A sport played on a field | A kind of food | *A lottery game | A children's game where you cover pictures on a card
